","H1612":"vine","H1818":"blood","H4325":"water","H8362":"to transplant","H6509":"be fruitful","H6058":"thickly branched","H7227":"many","H4294":"rod","H5797":"strength","H7626":"staff","H4910":"to rule","H1361":"to exult","H6967":"height","H5688":"cord","H1363":"height","H7230":"abundance","H1808":"branch","H5428":"to uproot","H2534":"rage","H7993":"to throw","H7307":"spirit","H6921":"east","H3001":"to wither","H6529":"fruit","H6561":"to tear","H784":"fire","H6258":"now","H4057":"mouth","H6723":"dryness","H6772":"thirst","H3318":"come","H905":"pole","H1931":"he/she/it"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",2,null,"challoner","Thy mother the lioness.... Jerusalem."],["challoner",3,null,"challoner","One of her whelps.... Viz., Joachaz, alias Sellum."],["challoner",5,null,"challoner","One of her young lions.... Joakim."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Princes: sons of Josias, who were so wretched. The latter part of this beautiful canticle, or allegory, (ver. 10.) regards Sedecias. (Calmet)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Lioness; Jerusalem (Challoner) which made alliances with the lions, or nations. (Calmet)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Whelps; Joachaz, or Sellum, (Challoner) who reigned only three months, like his brother Jechonias. (Haydock) — The four last kings were all cruel. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","But. Hebrew, “he was taken in their pit,” (Protestants; Haydock) as also [in] ver. 8. Septuagint, “in his or their corruption.” We do not read that Joachaz fought, (Calmet) though this passage insinuates as much. (Sanctius) (4 Kings xxiii. 30., &c.)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Lions: Joakim. (Challoner) — He reigned eleven years; but was such a monster, that the prophet does not speak of him or bewail his fate, 4 Kings xxiii. 34., and Jeremias xxii. 19. His words are applicable to Jechonias alone; who was cruel, and banished to Babylon, ver. 8. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Nations: rovers of Chaldea, Syria, &c., 4 Kings xxiv. 2. (Worthington) — Wounds. Hebrew, “pit.” He was besieged, (4 Kings xxiv. 11.; Calmet) and gave himself up. (Haydock)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Blood. She has given birth to many kings. (Menochius) — The original may be inaccurate, and perhaps should be, “a vine-tree or branch:” (Calmet) cormoc instead of bedammecha. Septuagint read kerimmon, “like a rose-flower,” planted, &c. (Haydock) — He speaks of Sedecias more obscurely. (Calmet) — The kingdom had flourished under David and Solomon. It afterwards lost its splendour under wicked kings, and all the tribes were removed. (Worthington)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Rods. The king had many children, and confided in them, but they were slain."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Dry; unfit for vine-trees. He speaks of the prison of Babylon. (Calmet) — The country was naturally wet. (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Fire. Ismael, who slew Godolias, was of the royal family, Jeremias xli., &c. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}